ing or  adding derived columns or just  flattening your uh nested fields  so we added like a lot of um  transform functions in pino natively for  doing just basic uh  column manipulations and then we also  have a way to write uh groovy scripts  for like a very custom transform logic  and uh to go back to the plugability  point it's very easy for you to just add  your own transform function  as a udf annotate it with one  pre-decided  keyword and then just drop it in  your deployment and you will just be  able to use it  and that becomes its own its own column  then  yes so this it becomes its own udf and  then you can use that in your uh when  you're setting up your table to say that  hey this column is going to use this udl  get this whole new value  and then the other cool thing to  help with this whole semi-structured  unstructured uh  shift  was  that we  are now natively able to just ingest  say nested json or arbitrary complex  json or just complex data structures  and once it is in pino  we have added this cool index called  json index  that lets you so it's going to go and  index every field in your complex  arbitrary block no matter what it is  and you will be able to  reap the same low latency query  performance of your complex json blob as  if it were a column that was  thought off and pre-processed and uh  put something that you would have put a  proper index on so you can just say hey  here's my json column i'm setting a json  index on it and you're good to go  wow  that sounds like black magic  yeah some of our benchmarks have shown  some amazing improvement when we use a  json index on a column versus just  having to  read it and then parse it and then do  some manipulation to extract something  so almost like going from tens of  seconds to a few milliseconds because of  adding json index  that's that's super interesting um well  on the index point because i wanted to  ask you about this i've noticed that  pinot supports so many different kinds  of indexes on  on columns which is like amazing um can  you talk about some of the most popular  ones  uh yes  so  on the same lines as json index in the  to help with this semi-structured  unstructured data we also have or text  index  this is  so you can just in add a text index on  say a very large text blob  and then you can do phrase prefix regex  based text search queries  and then we have geospatial indexes so  if you have  coordinates then you can just use  natively or geospatial index to run  geospatial queries uh this was actually  a great contribution by uh uber folks  again and  they built one very interesting feature  called orders near you in uber reads  okay  that uses this index  behind the scenes so when you kind of  open your uber app  you'll see in real time the orders that  are happening near you wow  so  all in real time like so you can eat you  can eat what your neighbor's eating yeah  and you can see what's popular in the  area which restaurant  is  which restaurant has the most orders  today and they have something special  got it yeah  uh so these three kind of help a lot  with just uh  log analytics text search or building  special dashboards and  that are special dashboards for  personalization or user facing analytics  like ubereats example  some of the other cool indexes would be  uh so we  started off with the forward index  inverted index the sorted index the very  basic ones  then we added range indexes which are  for  range queries so typically if you have  something like greater than this time  less than this time  then this index will uh make it much  faster than using a traditional inverted  index or a sorted index  and then  we also have the star tree index  which is uh  of course how the company star tree gets  its name from  and uh this one is a very cool index  uh so  this one is kind of going to help you so  think of this extreme to use two ends of  a spectrum where you have your data  and you could either choose to  not pre-aggregate anything at all just  keep everything unpre-aggregated and  then when you get a query that is when  you will go and do the  roll-ups  and then the aggregate it and then  compute your result when you get the  query  this is of course sometimes going to be  very slow especially if you have like a  lot of point lookup queries um  then you probably don't want to be in  that  side of the spectrum when you want to do  low latency user facing analytics  and then you have the other end of the  spectrum where you have  you can just pre-compute everything  this is typically what key value stores  do which is pre-computed  keep the result ready for every possible  combination  but then there's two problems with this  you've lost  flexibility  because every time there's a new  dimension or there's some new data you  have to make sure that it's pre-computed  otherwise you're not going to be able to  use that  and then also just be computing  everything is  going to result in space explosion  so star tree index is the solution to  this  because it lets you pick exactly which  columns matter to you and pre-aggregate  only those  pick exactly which aggregation functions  matter to you  and compute only those so you could say  that oh in my data set only the  dimensions country browser and device  these are the ones that i will do point  lookup queries on  so i'm only going to pre-aggregate these  i only care about some of this metrics  say sum of impressions  uh and then you can also specify that  at any point i want to make sure that  the query doesn't have to  pre do aggregations on more than say 10  000 records  okay so with this you are exactly able  to control  the amount of time that it's going to  spend in pre-computation versus what's  already there  and then you can choose to be wherever  you want on the spectrum of  no pre-computations to pre-compute  everything and then choose it based on  your space and latency trade-off  requirements very interesting and  where's the predicate handled in the  star tree index  uh  so  so think of it as uh if you have say in  the example that we did uh country  browser device  so we're going to start with like the  star node and then let's say we uh do  pre computations for like a few  countries and then we see that there's  only 10 000 records left so you might  start with u.s and canada  india china and then you know that oh  now this is my uh star note so  i got it  then you go next so you have like kind  of country so if you have a query like  uh select something from uh us  a country equal to us then it's going to  traverse the tree  uh it will say  oh in u.s and now i'm going to see the  next browser so and so or if you don't  have browser column then you'll go to  the star node under us and then pick the  uh  columns under those so all right and  then pre-compute those pre-compute those  those values those those aggregations  yes exactly  got it  um yeah it sounds super super powerful  and i've seen it i've seen the the  benchmarks on some of those queries and  they're very  very fast especially i'm sure when  they're tuned well  cool um well i know there's a couple  other  features that you've  introduced do you want to talk to us  about  and  um joint support and a couple other key  things  oh yes definitely so  upsets was one of the most impressive  features added to pino in the recent few  years so this feature is where when you  get an event  you insert it if it doesn't exist update  it if it does exist  so this was spearheaded by another  interesting use case that uber  engineering was trying to solve and this  clearly stretched the boundaries of what  pino can do  so the use case they were trying to  solve was something like  a fair update that happens after a trip  is over so you maybe add a tip or some  toll gets added so your fare is now  different  or let's say in  retail i've seen the use case where  there's this order and the state of the  order keeps changing  so you're going to get new events but  but while for some applications you  might just be concerned with the latest  event or the latest date  or you might have some special logic  that if i have several events with this  primary key i want to do something and  compute like a better value for it and  not just go with all the values  so  this was basically the upset feature and  because of this pinot has now uh  shifted from immutable data store to  supporting mutability  and  this is like a game changer for olap  systems uh which so far used to be  mutable or sorry so far used to be  immutable  so we're seeing many people in the  community doing very interesting things  with this feature  awesome  um and what about joins i think you've  made some change some changes to the  join um the the query uh engine uh on  the joint side  yes uh so in fact just today we have  published a blog about uh  joins in apache pino  yes this is a very recent feature it's  still in an early release branch  so  i mean i remember two years ago when  kishore came on uh  data council thursday as well  uh i remember him mentioning that we've  consciously stayed away from joints so  far  it's like a  design choice  this is kind of uh  we don't want to do this for low latency  queries and we have like a presto  connector ecosystem with which we can uh  so you can do the joins if you have  presto and then kind of  and then uh  we keep kept hearing from users that hey  we really love you know and we want it  to be the single store  uh  and also just having everything natively  in uh pinot is  uh nice  and uh  it it's  uh so kind of these two reasons  motivated us to get to adding joint  support and uh  this  we had to make some interesting changes  to the architecture of pinot to support  this  so  earlier on when i explained the broker  layer and the server layer it was pretty  much the single stage scattergather for  the longest time that's how it used to  be  and this is fine for analytical queries  like say filter and then aggregate and  then  when you send everything back to the  broker it has to do like limited merging  of results  but then when you get to multi-stage  queries like joins or this poses some  limitations because  data sets often won't be co-located so  after like initial filtering on all the  servers if you send everything back to  the brokers directly before any  aggregations  you might have to do some data shuffling  and then some local join and then  another final aggregation  and  a single stage  merge phase is going to easily become  like this bottleneck  so to  support this we have added a multi-stage  query execution support  and  there's like a lot more details about  how this is done uh in the blog that was  published today  and the cool thing is this not only  unlocks joins but also gives us the  ability to  add other complex multi-stage operations  like say window functions  so yeah this is this is one of the new  upcoming features that we are very  excited about that's a big update for  sure um  we'll make sure and share that blog post  our community and  and get it out via social um so that  people are aware of those changes but  that sounds especially significant  congrats  on that  great  well um uh neha it's been it's been so  great chatting with you um  we're out of time but i wanted to ask  you where can the community reach out to  you and find you if they have other  questions about pinot  uh so definitely the pinot slag channel  uh  that's the fastest and easiest way to  find me  uh  and also  you can find me on twitter at  nehapavar18  um sounds great well thanks for joining  us neha it was great to chat with you  and and hear about your enthusiasm and  and all of your commits uh on the pinot  project  same year it was great chatting with you  pete thank you awesome  and to our listeners thanks for joining  um we'll have more dc thursdays coming  up  and don't forget that you can check out  all of our dc thursday previous  conversations on our playlist on youtube  you can also see all the all the talks  from our recent data council austin on  our youtube channel as well  so  stay tuned
 

